* /**
*  * @file GIT.PULL
*  * @version 1.0
*  */
SUBROUTINE GIT.PULL
      $INCLUDE BP.INC PLATFORM.H
* PULL MATERIALISES, AND THAT DECIDES WHO CAN SERVE IT.
*
* A fast-forward writes the new commit's records back into the account, so pull
* needs record access.  The background process does not have it — mvgitd is
* built MVXGIT_NORECORDS precisely because the BASIC session owns the records
* (Model B) — and its record primitives call mv_fatal.  So `GIT PULL` over
* mvgitd did not fail: the daemon DIED mid-request, and the session sat waiting
* for a reply that could never arrive.  Over an hour of zero CPU time asleep in
* pipe_read, with no mvgitd left alive (mv_git#53).  A hang, not an error, which
* is why it never showed as a test failure — every run was killed by its timeout
* with the summary line never printed.
*
* uv-git and udt-git both have real record backends, and the CLI route has
* passed pull all along.  So the verb hands over, exactly as GIT CLONE does and
* for the same reason: this is the one shape of operation a session cannot serve
* for itself.  The developer still types GIT PULL and never sees a shell.
CALL GIT.SENT(S)
RNAME = FIELD(S, " ", 3)
BR = FIELD(S, " ", 4)
* One thing each, then ordinary runtime code — the nesting that silently
* compiled to nothing in GIT.CLONE (mv_git#52) is not worth repeating.
PLAT = ""
$IFDEF MVX
PLAT = "MVX"
$ENDIF
$IFDEF UDT
PLAT = "UDT"
$ENDIF
$IFDEF UV
PLAT = "UV"
$ENDIF
IF PLAT = "" THEN
   PRINT "GIT PULL: this build defines no platform - check BP.INC PLATFORM.H"
   RETURN
END
BEGIN CASE
   CASE PLAT = "MVX"
$IFDEF MVX
*     The engine IS the runtime here and reads the records itself.
      OUT = ""
      CALL GITPULL(".git", RNAME, BR, OUT)
      PRINT OUT
$ENDIF
   CASE 1
*     IN-SESSION, not a shell-out.  The git-object half (fetch, move the ref,
*     sync the git index) touches no records, so the background process can
*     still do it — PULLREF is PULL with the record write left out.  The records
*     are then written natively, here, exactly as GIT MERGE and GIT CHERRY-PICK
*     already do.
*
*     Handing over to uv-git/udt-git would also work and was tried first, but it
*     costs a SECOND session for the length of the command.  UniData TE licenses
*     two, so an in-session clone already exhausts them (mv_git#54) — and "one
*     licence where one will do" is the rule this whole design follows.
*     REPO first — dropping this when the handler was restructured cost a run:
*     UniVerse survived it (mvgitd resolves its own repo path when handed an
*     empty one) while UniData reported "UNINITIALIZED VARIABLE USED! Zero
*     Assumed!" and pulled nothing.  A thing that works on one platform by luck
*     is exactly what the other platform is for.
      CALL GIT.REPO(REPO)
*     BEFORE the ref moves, not after: PULLREF materialises the new HEAD itself,
*     and that includes removing records the tree does not carry.  Telling the
*     engine which VOC records are the system's only after PULLREF has run is
*     too late — the stock records are already deleted (mv_git#46).
      CALL GIT.OBJ("STOCKPREP", 1, REPO, "", "", "", "", OBJOUT)
      CALL GIT.OBJ("PULLREF", 3, REPO, RNAME, BR, "", "", OBJOUT)
      CALL GIT.ECHO(OBJOUT)             ;* fast-forward / up to date / conflict
      CALL GITUDT.CHECKOUT(REPO)        ;* materialise the new HEAD natively
END CASE
RETURN